  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-74424",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To estimate the probability that a randomly chosen student at the school has a gerbil and also has a rabbit, we\n\n- find the number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a gerbil\"** and the column **\"Has a rabbit\"**, and then\n\n- divide by the total number of data points in the table.\n\nThe number at the intersection of the row **\"Has a gerbil\"** and the column **\"Has a rabbit\"** is highlighted in the table below.\n\n|  | Has a rabbit | Doesn't have a rabbit | Totals |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Has a gerbil | $18$ | $12$ | $30$ |\n| Doesn't have a gerbil | $6$ | $36$ | $42$ |\n| Totals | $24$ | $48$ | $72$ |\n\nDividing by the total number of data points in the table $(72),$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{18}{72}=0.25.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
